# Control-Word Schema Generator

## 1. Purpose

The control-word schema generator creates a candidate logical control-word schema from the normalized output produced by the control-output extractor.

The generator converts:

```text
build/simulation_outputs/rom-generation/control-output-extractor/control-outputs.json
```

into:

```text
build/simulation_outputs/rom-generation/control-word-schema-generator/control-word-schema.candidate.json
```

The candidate output is intended for review and validation before its contents are incorporated into:

```text
rom/microcode/v1/control-word-schema.json
```

The generator does not directly overwrite the maintained specification.

## 2. Authority

The documentation under `docs/` remains authoritative.

The control-output extractor output is a generated representation of the documented control-output definitions. It is not an authoritative control specification.

The maintained control-word schema under `rom/microcode/v1/` contains reviewed generation decisions. It must not introduce fields, encodings, default values, or constraints that are unsupported by the authoritative documentation.

Relevant documentation includes:

- [Control Word](../../../docs/04-control/04-control-word.md)
- [Control Constraints](../../../docs/04-control/03-control-constraints.md)
- [Microarchitectural Control Signasl](../../../docs/04-control/20-control-output-definitions/01-microarchitectural-control-signals.md)
- [Architectural Control Signals](../../../docs/04-control/20-control-output-definitions/02-architectural-control-signals.md)
- [Sequencing Control Signals](../../../docs/04-control/20-control-output-definitions/03-sequencing-control-signals.md)

## 3. Scope

The generator is responsible for:

- reading normalized control-output extractor JSON
- validating the expected extractor-output structure
- creating one candidate schema entry for every extracted control field
- preserving documented field names
- preserving documented categories
- preserving documented widths
- preserving documented encodings
- preserving documented constraints
- preserving source references
- identifying an default value only when explicitly established by the extracted definition
- producing deterministic JSON output
- reporting incomplete or contradictory definitions

The generator is not responsible for:

- inventing missing field definitions
- inferring undocumented default values
- interpreting prose constraints as executable validation rules
- assigning logical bit positions
- assigning physical ROM bit positions
- mapping micro-operations to control fields
- selecting micro-operations
- expanding execution cases
- mapping control inputs to control words
- determining control-address packing
- generating ROM images

## 4. Inputs

The default input is:

```text
build/simulation_outputs/rom-generation/control-output-extractor/control-outputs.json
```

The input must have been generated by the control-output extractor from the current repository documentation.

The generator must not parse the control-output documentation directly. Extraction and normalization remain the responsibility of the control-output extractor.

The generator must reject input that:

- is missing
- is not valid UTF-8
- is not valid JSON
- does not match the supported extractor-output structure
- contains duplicate control-field names
- contains malformed field definitions
- uses an unsupported extractor schema version, when extractor schema versioning is available

## 5. Candidate Output

The default candidate output is:

```text
build/simulation_outputs/rom-generation/control-word-schema-generator/control-word-schema.candidate.json
```

The candidate output must use the following top-level structure:

```json
{
  "specification_type": "control-word-schema",
  "schema_version": 1,
  "control_design_version": "v1",
  "description": "Logical symbolic control-word schema for version 1 CPU control.",
  "fields": []
}
```

Each field entry must use the following structure:

```json
{
  "name": "FIELD_NAME",
  "category": "microarchitectural",
  "width": 1,
  "default_value": "0",
  "values": [
    {
      "encoding": "0",
      "name": "INACTIVE",
      "description": "No operation."
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [],
  "source": {
    "document": "docs/04-control/20-control-output-definitions/example.md",
    "section": "FIELD_NAME",
    "line": 1
  }
}
```

This example defines structure only. Its values must not be copied into generated entries unless they are present in the extractor input.

## 6. Transformation Rules

For each extracted control field, the generator must:

1. Create exactly one candidate field entry.
2. Preserve the documented field name.
3. Preserve the documented output category.
4. Preserve the normalized field width.
5. Preserve every documented legal encoding.
6. Preserve encoding names and descriptions when available.
7. Preserve documented field constraints without rewriting their meaning.
8. Preserve repository-relative source paths.
9. Preserve the applicable source section or definition identifier.
10. Preserve the source line when available.
11. Set `default_value` only from explicitly documented information.
12. Report missing required information rather than supplying a default.

The generator must not normalize distinct symbolic names into one name unless the documentation or extractor output explicitly establishes that equivalence.

The generator must not reorder encoding bits, convert between numeric bases, or alter encoding width.

## 7. Default-Value Handling

Every symbolic control field requires a canonical default value before complete control words can be generated.

The generator may assign `default_value` only when the extractor output explicitly identifies an encoding as default, disabled, unchanged, no operation, or another documented canonical default state.

The generator must not infer inactivity solely because an encoding is zero.

If no canonical default value can be established from the extracted definition:

- the field must remain identifiable in the candidate output
- the missing default value must be reported as an error
- the candidate must not be accepted as a complete maintained schema
- symbolic control-word generation must not proceed using that field

The diagnostic must identify the field and its source definition.

## 8. Source Traceability

Every candidate field must retain:

- repository-relative source document path
- source section or definition name
- source line when available

Source information must originate from the extractor output.

The generator must not construct absolute paths in the candidate specification.

Diagnostics must include source information when it is available.

## 9. Deterministic Output

Given identical input and configuration, the generator must produce identical output.

To support deterministic output:

- fields must use a documented stable ordering
- values within each field must use a documented stable ordering
- object properties must use a consistent order
- JSON indentation must be consistent
- output must use UTF-8
- output must end with one newline
- generated timestamps must not be included
- working-directory-specific paths must not be included

Unless the extractor establishes another required order, candidate fields should preserve extractor order. The implementation must document the selected rule.

## 10. Validation

The generator must validate at least:

- required top-level input properties
- supported input structure
- unique field names
- documented field categories
- positive field widths
- encoding values that fit the field width
- unique encodings within a field
- required encoding descriptions
- canonical default-value availability
- default values that match a documented legal encoding
- source-document presence
- source-section presence
- source-line representation when provided
- required candidate properties
- deterministic candidate ordering

The generator must report all independently detectable validation failures from one run when practical.

It must not write a candidate output that appears valid when errors prevent a complete schema from being constructed.

## 11. Diagnostics

Each diagnostic should contain:

- severity
- diagnostic code
- field name when applicable
- source document when available
- source line when available
- explanatory message

The generator should produce a human-readable report at:

```text
build/simulation_outputs/rom-generation/control-word-schema-generator/generation-report.txt
```

Errors prevent successful candidate generation.

Warnings identify review concerns that do not make the candidate structurally incomplete.

## 12. Maintained-Schema Workflow

The intended workflow is:

1. Update the authoritative control-output documentation.
2. Run the control-output extractor.
3. Review the extraction report.
4. Run the control-word schema generator.
5. Review the generation report.
6. Compare the candidate schema with the maintained schema.
7. Incorporate reviewed changes into `rom/microcode/v1/control-word-schema.json`.
8. Validate the maintained schema against the current extractor output.
9. Run applicable automated tests.

The generator must not automatically replace the maintained schema because extraction alone does not constitute review or approval of a maintained generation decision.

## 13. Command-Line Interface

The intended command form is:

```text
python tools/rom-generation/control-word-schema-generator/src/generate_control_word_schema.py --repo-root .
```

The command must support:

- an explicit repository root
- an optional alternate extractor-input path
- an optional alternate candidate-output path
- an optional alternate report-output path

Relative paths must be resolved from the supplied repository root.

The exact option names will be fixed during implementation and documented here.

## 14. Tests

Tests should cover representative behavior rather than every possible JSON permutation.

Required coverage includes:

- repository-relative path resolution
- alternate input and output paths
- missing input
- invalid UTF-8 input
- invalid JSON input
- malformed top-level structure
- duplicate field names
- category preservation
- width preservation
- encoding preservation
- constraint preservation
- source-traceability preservation
- explicitly documented default values
- missing default values
- invalid default values
- deterministic field ordering
- deterministic encoding ordering
- complete command-line execution
- candidate JSON generation
- diagnostic-report generation

Tests must use fixtures and must not depend on the repository’s current generated build artifacts.

## 15. Completion Boundary

The initial generator is complete when it can:

- consume valid control-output extractor JSON
- produce a complete candidate control-word schema
- preserve required source information
- reject incomplete or contradictory definitions
- produce deterministic JSON
- generate useful diagnostics
- pass its targeted automated tests

Micro-operation mapping, control-case expansion, address packing, and ROM-image generation belong to later stages.